// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Physics starts with a simple question: how do things move? Let's describe motion precisely.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Distance is how much ground you cover (a scalar, just a size). Displacement is your change in position, including direction (a vector)."},{"t":"key","text":"Average velocity = displacement ÷ time. Speed is the size of velocity, with no direction."},{"t":"ex","problem":"You walk 30 m east, then 10 m west, in 20 s. Find your distance, displacement and average velocity.","steps":["Distance: 30 + 10 = 40 m","Displacement: 30 − 10 = 20 m east","Velocity: 20 m ÷ 20 s"],"answer":"40 m, 20 m east, 1 m/s east"},{"t":"p","text":"On a position–time graph, the slope is the velocity. A straight line means constant velocity; a curve means the velocity is changing."}]},{"say":"Acceleration means velocity is changing: speeding up, slowing down, or turning.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time: a = (v_f − v_i) ÷ t. Units: m/s²."},{"t":"p","text":"An acceleration of 3 m/s² means the velocity changes by 3 m/s every second. Slowing down is acceleration too: its direction is opposite the motion."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A car goes from 10 m/s to 30 m/s in 5 s. Find its acceleration.","steps":["a = (30 − 10) ÷ 5"],"answer":"4 m/s²"},{"t":"p","text":"On a velocity–time graph, the slope is the acceleration and the area under the line is the displacement."},{"t":"oops","text":"An object can have zero velocity and still accelerate, like a ball at the very top of its toss."}]},{"say":"When acceleration is constant, four equations let you predict exactly where something will be.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"v = v₀ + at · Δx = v₀t + ½at² · v² = v₀² + 2aΔx · Δx = ½(v₀ + v)t"},{"t":"p","text":"Pick the equation that has the three things you know and the one you want."},{"t":"p","text":"Free fall is motion under gravity alone. Near Earth, every object falls with acceleration g = 9.8 m/s² downward (ignoring air resistance), no matter its mass."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A rock is dropped from rest off a cliff. How far does it fall in 3 s?","steps":["v₀ = 0, a = 9.8 m/s², t = 3 s","Δx = ½(9.8)(3²) = 4.9 × 9"],"answer":"44.1 m"}]}],[{"say":"Why do you lurch forward when a bus stops? Newton's first law explains it.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Newton's first law: an object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion stays in motion at constant velocity, unless a net force acts on it."},{"t":"p","text":"Inertia is the tendency to resist changes in motion. More mass means more inertia."},{"t":"p","text":"A force is a push or pull, measured in newtons (N). The net force is the sum of all forces on an object. If forces are balanced (net force = 0), the object's velocity doesn't change."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A book rests on a table. Gravity pulls down with 10 N. What's the net force?","steps":["The table pushes up with 10 N (normal force)","10 N up − 10 N down"],"answer":"0 N, so the book stays at rest"}]},{"say":"Newton's second law is the most useful equation in physics. It links force, mass and acceleration.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"F_net = ma. Acceleration is in the direction of the net force."},{"t":"p","text":"A bigger force gives more acceleration; a bigger mass gives less. 1 newton is the force that accelerates 1 kg at 1 m/s²."},{"t":"p","text":"Weight is the force of gravity on an object: W = mg. A 50 kg student weighs 50 × 9.8 = 490 N. Mass stays the same everywhere; weight changes on the Moon."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 1,200 kg car accelerates at 3 m/s². What net force acts on it?","steps":["F = ma = 1,200 × 3"],"answer":"3,600 N"}]},{"say":"Forces always come in pairs. And friction is the force that makes walking possible!","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Newton's third law: for every action force there is an equal and opposite reaction force, acting on a different object."},{"t":"p","text":"When you push on a wall, the wall pushes back on you just as hard. A rocket pushes gas down; the gas pushes the rocket up."},{"t":"p","text":"Friction opposes sliding. Static friction keeps things from starting to move; kinetic friction acts on things already sliding. Friction force = μ × normal force, where μ is the coefficient of friction."},{"t":"p","text":"A free-body diagram shows an object as a dot with arrows for every force on it: gravity (down), normal force (perpendicular to the surface), friction (opposite motion), tension and applied forces."},{"t":"oops","text":"Action–reaction forces never cancel, because they act on different objects."}]}],[{"say":"In two dimensions, direction really matters. Vectors let us add motions and forces at angles.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A vector has a magnitude and a direction, drawn as an arrow. To add vectors, place them tip to tail; the resultant goes from the first tail to the last tip."},{"t":"p","text":"Any vector can be split into components: x = V cos θ and y = V sin θ."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A plane flies 300 km north, then 400 km east. Find its displacement.","steps":["The legs are perpendicular, so use the Pythagorean theorem","√(300² + 400²) = √250,000"],"answer":"500 km, at about 53° east of north"},{"t":"p","text":"Perpendicular components are independent: what happens horizontally doesn't affect what happens vertically."}]},{"say":"Throw a ball and it follows a curved path. Split it into horizontal and vertical parts and it gets simple!","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"A projectile moves at constant horizontal velocity while accelerating downward at 9.8 m/s². The two motions are independent."},{"t":"p","text":"A ball rolled off a table and a ball dropped from the same height hit the floor at the same time, because their vertical motions are identical."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A ball is thrown horizontally at 15 m/s from a 20 m cliff. How far from the base does it land?","steps":["Vertical: 20 = ½(9.8)t², so t ≈ 2.0 s","Horizontal: x = 15 × 2.0"],"answer":"about 30 m"},{"t":"p","text":"A projectile launched from the ground goes farthest at 45° (without air resistance)."}]},{"say":"Anything moving in a circle is always accelerating toward the center. Gravity keeps the planets doing exactly that!","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Even at constant speed, an object moving in a circle is accelerating because its direction keeps changing. This centripetal acceleration points toward the center: a = v²/r."},{"t":"p","text":"The force causing it is the centripetal force: F = mv²/r. It can come from tension, friction or gravity."},{"t":"key","text":"Newton's law of universal gravitation: F = Gm₁m₂/r². Double the distance and gravity becomes 1/4 as strong."},{"t":"p","text":"Gravity provides the centripetal force that keeps the Moon and satellites in orbit. Astronauts float because they're falling around Earth along with their spacecraft."},{"t":"oops","text":"There's no outward \"centrifugal force\" pushing you out on a merry-go-round. Your inertia wants to go straight, and the ride pulls you inward."}]}],[{"say":"In physics, work has a very specific meaning: a force moving something.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Work = force × distance (in the direction of the force): W = Fd. Units: joules (J)."},{"t":"p","text":"If you push on a wall and it doesn't move, you do no work on it. Carrying a box horizontally at constant speed does no work on the box either, since the force (up) is perpendicular to the motion."},{"t":"p","text":"Power is how fast work is done: P = W ÷ t, measured in watts (W). 1 W = 1 J/s."},{"t":"ex","problem":"You lift a 100 N box 2 m in 4 s. Find the work and power.","steps":["W = 100 × 2 = 200 J","P = 200 ÷ 4"],"answer":"200 J of work at 50 W"},{"t":"p","text":"Simple machines (levers, pulleys, ramps) don't reduce work; they let you use less force over a longer distance."}]},{"say":"Energy comes in different forms. Moving things have kinetic energy, and lifted things store potential energy.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Kinetic energy KE = ½mv². Gravitational potential energy PE = mgh."},{"t":"p","text":"Kinetic energy depends on speed squared: doubling speed quadruples KE. That's why highway crashes are so much worse than slow ones."},{"t":"p","text":"Gravitational potential energy depends on height. Elastic potential energy is stored in stretched or compressed springs."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find the KE of a 2 kg ball moving at 3 m/s and the PE of a 2 kg ball on a 5 m shelf.","steps":["KE = ½(2)(3²) = 9 J","PE = 2 × 9.8 × 5"],"answer":"KE 9 J, PE 98 J"}]},{"say":"Energy can't be created or destroyed. It just changes form. This is one of the deepest laws in all of science.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Law of conservation of energy: the total energy of a closed system stays constant."},{"t":"p","text":"On a frictionless roller coaster, potential energy at the top turns into kinetic energy at the bottom: mgh = ½mv²."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A ball is dropped from 5 m. How fast is it moving just before it hits the ground?","steps":["mgh = ½mv², so v = √(2gh)","v = √(2 × 9.8 × 5) = √98"],"answer":"about 9.9 m/s"},{"t":"p","text":"In real life, friction turns some energy into thermal energy (heat), so mechanical energy decreases, but the total energy is still conserved."},{"t":"p","text":"Efficiency = useful energy out ÷ energy in × 100%. No machine is 100% efficient."}]}],[{"say":"A slow truck and a fast baseball can both be hard to stop. Momentum measures how hard.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Momentum p = mv. It's a vector, in the direction of motion. Units: kg·m/s."},{"t":"p","text":"Impulse is a force applied over time: J = FΔt. Impulse equals the change in momentum: FΔt = Δp."},{"t":"p","text":"To reduce the force in a collision, increase the time it takes to stop. That's how airbags, helmets, and bending your knees when you land all work."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 0.15 kg baseball moving at 40 m/s is caught and stopped in 0.1 s. What force does the glove apply?","steps":["Δp = 0.15 × 40 = 6 kg·m/s","F = Δp ÷ Δt = 6 ÷ 0.1"],"answer":"60 N"}]},{"say":"When objects collide, momentum is passed between them, but the total stays the same.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"In a closed system, total momentum before a collision equals total momentum after."},{"t":"list","items":["Elastic collisions: objects bounce apart and kinetic energy is conserved too (like billiard balls, nearly).","Inelastic collisions: some kinetic energy turns into heat and sound.","Perfectly inelastic: objects stick together."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 2 kg cart at 3 m/s hits a 1 kg cart at rest. They stick together. Find their speed.","steps":["Before: 2 × 3 + 1 × 0 = 6 kg·m/s","After: (2 + 1)v = 6"],"answer":"v = 2 m/s"},{"t":"p","text":"Recoil is conservation of momentum too: when a cannon fires a ball forward, the cannon moves backward."}]}],[{"say":"Waves carry energy from place to place without carrying matter. Sound, light and ocean waves are all waves!","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Mechanical waves need a medium (sound, water waves). Electromagnetic waves don't (light).","Transverse waves vibrate perpendicular to the direction they travel (light, waves on a rope).","Longitudinal waves vibrate parallel to the direction of travel (sound), with compressions and rarefactions."]},{"t":"p","text":"Parts of a wave: wavelength (λ, distance between crests), amplitude (height, related to energy), frequency (f, waves per second in hertz) and period (T = 1/f)."},{"t":"key","text":"Wave speed = frequency × wavelength: v = fλ."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A wave has frequency 5 Hz and wavelength 2 m. Find its speed.","steps":["v = fλ = 5 × 2"],"answer":"10 m/s"}]},{"say":"Waves bounce, bend and add up in fascinating ways. Let's see what happens when they meet obstacles and each other.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Reflection: a wave bounces off a surface (an echo).","Refraction: a wave bends when it changes speed entering a new medium.","Diffraction: a wave spreads out around obstacles or through openings (you can hear around corners).","Interference: when waves overlap, they add. Constructive interference makes a bigger wave; destructive interference can cancel them out (noise-cancelling headphones)."]},{"t":"p","text":"A standing wave forms when waves reflect back and forth and interfere, creating nodes (no motion) and antinodes (maximum motion). Guitar strings vibrate as standing waves."},{"t":"p","text":"Resonance happens when something is pushed at its natural frequency, building large vibrations, like pushing a swing at just the right moment."}]},{"say":"Sound is vibrations traveling through the air. Its pitch and loudness depend on the wave.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sound travels about 343 m/s in air, faster in liquids, and fastest in solids. It can't travel through a vacuum."},{"t":"list","items":["Pitch depends on frequency: high frequency = high pitch. Humans hear about 20 to 20,000 Hz.","Loudness depends on amplitude and is measured in decibels (dB). Every 10 dB is 10 times the intensity."]},{"t":"key","text":"Doppler effect: a source moving toward you sounds higher-pitched; moving away, it sounds lower."},{"t":"ex","problem":"You see lightning, then hear thunder 3 s later. About how far away is it?","steps":["distance = speed × time","343 × 3"],"answer":"about 1,000 m (1 km)"}]}],[{"say":"Visible light is just a tiny slice of a huge family of waves called the electromagnetic spectrum.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Electromagnetic waves are vibrating electric and magnetic fields. They travel through a vacuum at the speed of light, c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s."},{"t":"key","text":"From longest wavelength (lowest energy) to shortest (highest energy): radio, microwaves, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays."},{"t":"p","text":"Visible light runs from red (longest) to violet (shortest): ROY G BIV. White light is a mix of all colors."},{"t":"p","text":"We see an object's color because it reflects that color and absorbs the others. A red apple reflects red light."},{"t":"p","text":"Light acts as both a wave and a particle. Particles of light are called photons; higher frequency photons carry more energy."}]},{"say":"Mirrors work because of one simple rule about how light bounces.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Law of reflection: the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, both measured from the normal."},{"t":"list","items":["Plane (flat) mirrors make upright, virtual images the same size as the object, as far behind the mirror as the object is in front.","Concave mirrors curve inward and can focus light to a point (the focal point). They make real or virtual images, used in telescopes and makeup mirrors.","Convex mirrors curve outward and spread light, giving a wider view with smaller images (car side mirrors)."]},{"t":"p","text":"A real image can be projected on a screen; a virtual image can't, because light only appears to come from it."}]},{"say":"A straw looks bent in a glass of water. That's refraction: light bending as it changes speed.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Light slows down in materials like water and glass. The index of refraction n = c ÷ v tells how much. When light enters a slower medium at an angle, it bends toward the normal."},{"t":"p","text":"Total internal reflection happens when light in a slower medium hits the boundary at a large angle and reflects completely. It's how fiber-optic cables carry internet signals."},{"t":"list","items":["Convex (converging) lenses are thicker in the middle and focus light. Magnifying glasses, cameras and your eye use them.","Concave (diverging) lenses are thinner in the middle and spread light. They correct nearsightedness."]},{"t":"p","text":"A prism separates white light into colors because each color refracts by a slightly different amount (dispersion). Rainbows work the same way."}]}],[{"say":"Rub a balloon on your hair and it sticks to the wall. That's electric charge at work!","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"There are two kinds of charge: positive (protons) and negative (electrons). Like charges repel; opposite charges attract."},{"t":"p","text":"Objects become charged by gaining or losing electrons: by friction (rubbing), conduction (touching) or induction (bringing a charge near without touching). Charge is conserved: it's moved, never created."},{"t":"key","text":"Coulomb's law: the electric force gets stronger with more charge and weaker with distance: F = kq₁q₂/r²."},{"t":"p","text":"Conductors (metals) let charges move easily; insulators (rubber, plastic, glass) don't. Lightning is a giant static discharge."}]},{"say":"Electricity in a circuit is like water in pipes. Voltage is the push, current is the flow, and resistance slows it down.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Current (I) is the flow of charge, measured in amperes (A).","Voltage (V) is the electric potential difference that pushes charges, measured in volts.","Resistance (R) opposes current, measured in ohms (Ω). Thinner, longer or hotter wires have more resistance."]},{"t":"key","text":"Ohm's law: V = IR. Electrical power: P = IV."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 12 V battery is connected to a 4 Ω resistor. Find the current and power.","steps":["I = V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 A","P = IV = 3 × 12"],"answer":"3 A and 36 W"},{"t":"p","text":"Your electric bill charges for energy in kilowatt-hours: power (kW) × time (h)."}]},{"say":"How you connect parts of a circuit changes everything. Let's compare series and parallel.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A circuit needs a closed loop with an energy source. An open switch breaks the loop."},{"t":"list","items":["Series circuit: one path. Current is the same everywhere. Resistances add: R_total = R₁ + R₂ + … If one bulb burns out, they all go out.","Parallel circuit: several branches. Voltage is the same across each branch. 1/R_total = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + … Adding branches lowers total resistance. If one bulb burns out, the others stay on. Homes are wired in parallel."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Two 6 Ω resistors are connected (a) in series and (b) in parallel. Find each total resistance.","steps":["Series: 6 + 6 = 12 Ω","Parallel: 1/R = 1/6 + 1/6 = 1/3"],"answer":"12 Ω in series, 3 Ω in parallel"},{"t":"p","text":"Fuses and circuit breakers protect circuits by opening when the current gets too high."}]}],[{"say":"Magnets and electricity are two sides of the same coin. A moving charge makes a magnetic field!","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Magnets have north and south poles. Like poles repel and unlike poles attract. Magnetic field lines go from north to south outside the magnet. Earth itself is a giant magnet, which is why compasses work."},{"t":"key","text":"An electric current creates a magnetic field. This is electromagnetism."},{"t":"p","text":"An electromagnet is a coil of wire around an iron core. Turning the current on or off turns the magnet on or off, and more coils or more current make it stronger."},{"t":"p","text":"Electric motors use magnetic forces on current-carrying wires to turn electrical energy into motion."}]},{"say":"If electricity can make magnetism, can magnetism make electricity? Yes! That's how power plants work.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Electromagnetic induction: a changing magnetic field through a coil creates a voltage and current in the coil."},{"t":"p","text":"Moving a magnet into a coil of wire induces a current; moving it out reverses the current. The faster the change, the bigger the voltage."},{"t":"p","text":"A generator spins coils in a magnetic field (turned by steam, wind or water) to make electricity. It's a motor run in reverse."},{"t":"p","text":"A transformer uses induction to change voltage. Power lines carry electricity at very high voltage to reduce energy loss, then transformers step it down for homes."},{"t":"p","text":"Alternating current (AC) changes direction many times per second (60 Hz in the US). Direct current (DC), from batteries, flows one way."}]},{"say":"Around 1900, physicists discovered that tiny things follow strange new rules. Welcome to modern physics!","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Energy comes in tiny packets called quanta. Light's photons each carry energy E = hf, so higher frequency light carries more energy per photon."},{"t":"p","text":"The photoelectric effect (light knocking electrons off metal) showed that light behaves like particles. Einstein won the Nobel Prize for explaining it."},{"t":"p","text":"Electrons in atoms can only have certain energy levels. When they drop to a lower level, they emit a photon of a specific color, giving each element its own spectrum."},{"t":"key","text":"E = mc²: mass and energy are equivalent. A tiny amount of mass can release an enormous amount of energy, which is what powers nuclear reactions and the Sun."},{"t":"p","text":"Nuclear fission splits heavy nuclei (used in power plants); fusion combines light nuclei (in stars)."}]}]];
export default text;
